Requirements
------------
AmigaOS 3.1 or newer, a 68000 or better, no FPU. Amiga Installer 43 or
newer: OS 3.2 has SYS:System/Installer; for OS 3.1 use Installer 43.3
(Aminet util/misc; the Installer on the OS 3.1 Install disk is older).
If Workbench cannot find the installer, set the Install icon's Default
Tool to its full path.

Installing
----------
Double-click Install, or from a Shell in this drawer:

  Installer Install MINUSER AVERAGE

The installer first shows what it found: an existing MUI (3.x, 4 or 5 in
SYS:MUI or MUI:, its lines in S:User-Startup), every muimaster.library in
the LIBS: drawers, and an earlier OpenMUI installation. None of it is
changed. OpenMUI goes into the first LIBS: drawer (normally SYS:Libs),
which programs search before MUI's MUI:Libs. S:Startup-Sequence and
S:User-Startup are not changed.

  Libs/muimaster.library -> first LIBS: drawer
  Libs/OpenMUI/*         -> LIBS:OpenMUI/ (class files and modules,
                            loaded when a program first needs them)
  Libs/MUI/*             -> LIBS:MUI/ of the first LIBS: drawer, never
                            into a real MUI's own MUI:Libs/mui
  Prefs/OpenMUI          -> SYS:Prefs/OpenMUI
  Locale/Catalogs/*      -> LOCALE:Catalogs/<language>/ (optional)
  Tools/OpenMUISwitch and the icons -> SYS:OpenMUI

Every file the installer replaces is kept in SYS:OpenMUI/Data/Backup;
every file it installs is recorded in SYS:OpenMUI/Data/Manifest.

Bundled MUI classes (NList, TextEditor, TheBar, UP-MCC and others) are
installed only when no same or newer version is in a LIBS:MUI drawer; an
older one is kept in the backup. Classes of a real MUI (MUI:Libs/mui)
belong to that MUI and are not used by OpenMUI, which has its own copies.
The last installer page lists what was done with each.

Running Install again offers "Install this version over it" (an update:
the earlier installation is removed, your files put back, the new one
installed; settings are kept) and "Remove OpenMUI".

An installation of an earlier test build is migrated: its settings in
ENVARC:Zune and ENV:Zune move to ENVARC:OpenMUI and ENV:OpenMUI (a file
already there is not replaced), and its files leave LIBS:Zune. Other
files in LIBS:Zune are kept in the backup and returned by Uninstall and
"Use real MUI".

Novice installs everything without questions; Intermediate and Expert
ask whether to install the translations. Pretend mode changes nothing.

Use real MUI, Use OpenMUI, Uninstall
------------------------------------
Icons in SYS:OpenMUI (Use real MUI and Use OpenMUI only when a real MUI
was found):

  Use real MUI  moves OpenMUI's libraries and classes to
                SYS:OpenMUI/Data/Parked and puts back your own files they
                replaced. After a reboot programs get your MUI.
  Use OpenMUI   the reverse; after a reboot programs get OpenMUI.
  Uninstall     deletes every file OpenMUI installed, restores every
                file of yours it replaced, removes the drawers it made
                when empty. Asks whether to keep ENVARC:OpenMUI.

From a Shell: SYS:OpenMUI/Data/OpenMUISwitch MUI, OPENMUI, UNINSTALL
[SETTINGS] or STATUS (what is installed where).

Settings
--------
SYS:Prefs/OpenMUI is the preferences program. Settings are in
ENVARC:OpenMUI (saved) and ENV:OpenMUI (in use). MUI's own settings
(ENVARC:MUI) are not changed. Class files are in LIBS:OpenMUI.

A program written for MUI 3 sees MUI 3.8 (muimaster.library 19.35), one
asking for MUI 4 sees MUI 4, one asking for MUI 5 sees MUI 5 (21.227).
Preferences, System page, "MUI version": "Behave as" Automatic (default),
MUI 3.8, MUI 4 or MUI 5 for all programs, and a list of programs (by file
name) with a MUI of their own. Many programs written for MUI 4/5 ask for
version 19 and so get MUI 3.8 under Automatic: give such a program MUI 5
in that list.

Status
------
muimaster.library [VERSION], dated [RELEASE]. Emulator measurements (A1200,
OS 3.1, 4 MB fast RAM): MUI-Demo needs 428 KB for the first program (MUI
3.8: 460 KB) and 193 KB for each further one (MUI 3.8: 211-219 KB). Not
measured on a real Amiga. Reports: https://github.com/creep-ltx/openmui

Developing with the SDK
-----------------------
Add SDK/include to the include path and link with -lmui:

  m68k-amigaos-gcc -noixemul -Os -I../include -o HelloZune HelloZune.c -lmui

